# Timeouts for agents and summarizers, and a cap on provider output line size

## What to build

Two robustness fixes in the agent-process layer (bug reports KAN-66 / GE-05 and KAN-73 / GE-12).

**1. Idle timeout (GE-05).** Today nothing stops an agent on a clock: `src/providers/spawn.ts` says so in its doc comment (lines 47–49, "Agents run until they exit or are aborted; nothing stops them on a clock"), and a provider that hangs makes the run wait forever. Reproduce: `GRAPH_EXECUTION_FAKE_SCRIPT` = `{"task:1":{"attempts":[{"hang":true}]}}` → task 1 stays `running` forever. The failure summarizer (`src/agents/summarizer.ts` lines 94–118) has the same problem, and it is worse there: `run.ts` line 419 does `await summaries.drain()` before the run ends, so one hung summarizer blocks the end of the whole run.

Add a new config key `idleTimeoutMinutes` (number, `>= 0`, fractions allowed, default `20`; `0` turns it off), validated in the zod schema in `src/config/config.ts`. A new provider wrapper `watchIdle(provider, minutes, onIdle)` in a new file `src/agents/idle.ts` stops an agent that produces **no event** (no output line, no tool call) for that long: its process tree is killed exactly as on an abort (its own `AbortController`, which `spawnAgent` turns into a process-group kill) and its `done` rejects with `IdleTimeoutError`. `withRetries` (`src/agents/retry.ts`) counts that as a failed attempt of a new class `idle`, so the next attempt starts fresh and is told why, exactly like the existing loop detection (`watchLoops`, commit 9a9d475). It is wired in every place `watchLoops` is: `src/runner/run.ts` (covers task, resolver, reviewer, tester, fixer **and** the failure summarizer, which uses the same wrapped provider), `src/suite/e2e.ts` and `src/suite/integrate.ts`. In addition the summarizer gets a hard wall-clock cap (`SUMMARY_TIMEOUT_MS`, 5 minutes) inside `runFailureSummary`, so a summarizer can never hold up the end of a run, whatever the config says. Config keys are NOT mirrored by CLI flags in this repo (`attempts`, `loopDetection`, `keepRuns` … are config-only; only `--concurrency`, `--model` and `--human-review` exist as flags), so add no CLI flag.

Why 20 minutes and "idle" rather than a total limit: a real agent legitimately runs for an hour, and Claude Code's `stream-json` prints nothing while one long tool call (a slow test suite or build) runs, so the timeout counts silence, and 20 minutes leaves room for a long test run. Document this in the README.

**2. Output line cap (GE-12).** Providers read the agent's output with execa's line iterator (`agent.child.iterable({ from: "all" })`). execa accumulates a line until it sees `\n`, so one line with no newline grows memory without bound (checked: a child writing 64 MiB without a newline yields one 67,108,864-character string). Fix it in `spawnAgent`: give execa a binary **transform** on `stdout` and on `stderr` (`capLines()`) that splits lines itself and truncates any single line longer than `MAX_LINE_CHARS = 1 << 20` (1 Mi characters), replacing the rest with ` [truncated N characters]`. Memory per stream then stays at most one capped line plus one chunk. Also `readLogTail` (`src/agents/summarizer.ts` lines 66–76), which builds the failure summary's log tail, today reads the agent's **whole** log file into memory (`readFile` of `logs/<label>.log`). That file collects the output of every attempt, so each failure copies everything the agent ever printed. On a log over ~512 MB `readFile` throws and the tail comes back empty. Change it to read only the last 64 KiB of the file.

## Context: files to read before implementing

